# The Paper at a Glance

## How the paper is set

The paper carries **100 marks** in three hours. **Part I** has twenty MCQs for twenty marks, answered on a separate sheet in at most thirty minutes. **Part II** has seven questions, numbered 2 to 8, of which you attempt **four**, each worth twenty marks. One question is usually split into short notes.

@py topic_weights

@py heatmap_topics

## The time plan

::: table | A working time plan for the three hours.
@widths 3,2,9
Stage | Minutes | What you do
Part I (MCQs) | 20–25 | Answer all twenty; mark doubtful ones and return to them; never leave a blank
Choosing | 8–10 | Read all seven questions twice; choose the four where you have theory *and* Pakistan evidence
Answer maps | 4 × 3 | For each question, a quick map: introduction, numbered headings, evidence, diagram, conclusion
Writing | 4 × 30 | About 30 minutes per answer: 1,000–1,300 words
Review | 5–8 | Underline key terms; check names, dates and numbers; number the headings clearly
:::

## The frame of every scoring answer

::: fig flow | The frame of a scoring answer.
@per 5
Introduction: a simple definition and your argument
Headings: numbered, taken from the question's own words
Each heading: a point, then a thinker, law or dated figure
A diagram: early, labelled, referred to in the text
Conclusion: a judgement and a way forward for Pakistan
:::

::: remember | Five habits the examiner rewards
1. **Decode every limb.** "Define, discuss and differentiate" is three tasks; answer all three under separate headings.
2. **Evidence inside the point.** Every heading carries a name with a date, a law with its year, or a figure with its source.
3. **Pakistan in every answer.** Even a Western-theory question ends with what it explains in Pakistan.
4. **One diagram at least.** The key diagrams in these notes are chosen because they fit the questions that repeat.
5. **A judgement, not a summary.** End with a line to take — each revision sheet gives one for every heading.
:::

## What is due

The record shows a clear pattern: the examiner returns to the families that repeat, but every year adds at least one question from a line never yet asked in full. Prepare an answer plan for each line below.

::: table | The due list: syllabus lines never yet asked as a full question (or silent for three years).
@widths 1.2,12.8
Topic | Lines that are due
T1 | The discipline half — Women's versus Gender Studies, autonomy and integration, status in Pakistan — silent since 2023
T2 | Liberal feminism; men's feminism
T3 | The UN conferences and CEDAW as a full question (only MCQs so far)
T4 | World-system theory on its own; gender mainstreaming; the SDGs; the care economy
T5 | Women's education; family law and the MFLO 1961; women in politics; climate impacts on women
T6 | Defining governance; women as voters; the South Asian experience
T7 | Acid attacks; workplace harassment; cyber violence; the ecological model; a comparison of the three case studies
:::

## The links between topics

The same ideas serve many questions. Learn each once, in its home topic, and carry it across.

::: table | Ideas that cross topics.
@widths 4.4,1.6,8
Idea | Home | Also earns marks in
Intersectionality (Crenshaw 1989; Collins 1990) | T2 | T5 marginalised groups (2026); T7 root causes; T1 the move from Women's to Gender Studies
Social construction; performativity (Butler 1990) | T1 | T2 postmodern feminism; T7 masculinities and violence
Hegemonic masculinity (Connell 1987, 1995) | T1 | T7 masculinity studies (2026); T2 men's feminism and men's movements
Patriarchy (Millett 1970; Walby 1990) | T2 | T7 root causes of violence; T5 status of women
CEDAW (1979; Pakistan 1996) | T3 | T5 law and human rights; T6 Article 7 and quotas; T7 General Recommendation 19
Beijing Platform (1995) | T3 | T4 gender mainstreaming; T6 Critical Area G; T7 Critical Area D
Boserup (1970) and WID | T4 | T5 women's employment; T1 why a discipline was needed
Moser's triple role; Hochschild's second shift | T4 · T5 | T5 the double burden (2019); T4 globalisation
Hudood Ordinances (1979) and WAF (1981) | T3 | T5 law; T7 laws on violence
Global Gender Gap Report (2025: 148th of 148; 2026: 143rd of 145) | T5 | T1 significance of Gender Studies; T6 political empowerment
Reserved seats and the LFO (2002) | T6 | T3 phase five of the movement; T5 women in politics
Honour-killing law (2016) | T7 | T5 law and human rights; T1 the honour-killing example of two lenses
Malala Yousafzai | T7 | T5 girls' education
NGO-isation | T3 | T4 donor-driven projects and the WAD critique
Structural violence (Galtung 1969) | T7 | T5 the enforcement gap; T4 dependency and world-system
:::

